    {
      "id": "b695b85c-98f0-4258-857a-6710298d1e05",
      "name": "Why MCP is Awesome - Part 1",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Why MCP is Awesome - Part 1",
      "docTypeId": "0760eea0-e943-476d-bc95-228b911a5d3a",
      "templateId": null,
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "Why MCP is Awesome - Part 1"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-12 09:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "MCP (Model-Content-Platform) revolutionizes how we think about web development by separating concerns and enabling true reusability across projects."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "listingImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "d3bacfe8-4e3a-44ff-9883-7878b159c165",
              "mediaKey": "09dd3fe1-3035-417d-825c-5689494cd7cc",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "body",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "9cd4324e-6613-4157-8bd9-ca6974469aea",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "0d1ba22d-8239-42ce-826a-36ffa491b706",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "a5fd9492-5855-4e0f-8083-763710afbb14",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "159721a2-3f31-47b9-8e4e-7510d4d3c130",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "8ece1380-7b5c-4ff8-af21-c0405e60812b",
                "key": "9cd4324e-6613-4157-8bd9-ca6974469aea",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>MCP (Model-Content-Platform) represents a paradigm shift in web development. By separating the model layer from the content layer and leveraging a powerful platform to orchestrate them, developers can build applications that are truly reusable across projects and teams.</p><p>In this series, we'll explore how MCP enables:</p><ul><li>Clean separation of concerns</li><li>Reusable components and patterns</li><li>Better maintainability and scalability</li><li>Improved developer experience</li></ul>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "7fa92327-7ebc-4c31-91bf-c60dc3fb01fd",
                "key": "0d1ba22d-8239-42ce-826a-36ffa491b706",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "a1b2c3d4-5678-90ab-cdef-1234567890ab",
                        "mediaKey": "f4c54555-ebcb-4db5-9ad5-d56603d99d35",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "caption",
                    "value": "MCP Architecture Diagram - showing the separation between Model, Content, and Platform layers"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "8ece1380-7b5c-4ff8-af21-c0405e60812b",
                "key": "a5fd9492-5855-4e0f-8083-763710afbb14",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>The key insight behind MCP is that your data model (the \"Model\") should be completely independent from how you present and manage content (the \"Content\"). The \"Platform\" then provides the orchestration layer that connects them.</p><p>This separation allows you to:</p><ul><li>Change your frontend without touching your data model</li><li>Reuse the same content structure across multiple projects</li><li>Have different teams work on model and content independently</li><li>Easily migrate between different CMS platforms</li></ul><p>In Umbraco specifically, MCP enables you to build truly reusable block types and compositions that work across any site using the same patterns.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "ae448a70-e946-44f6-94d0-31218f039712",
                "key": "159721a2-3f31-47b9-8e4e-7510d4d3c130",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "quote",
                    "value": "MCP isn't just a pattern - it's a mindset shift that changes how you approach every aspect of web development from architecture to deployment."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "attribution",
                    "value": "Claude Code, Umbraco.Bench Project"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "9cd4324e-6613-4157-8bd9-ca6974469aea",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "0d1ba22d-8239-42ce-826a-36ffa491b706",
