    {
      "id": "e75fdbb0-1c92-4616-8c7f-897199ef0a7e",
      "name": "Why MCP is Awesome - Part 2",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Why MCP is Awesome - Part 2",
      "docTypeId": "0760eea0-e943-476d-bc95-228b911a5d3a",
      "templateId": null,
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "Why MCP is Awesome - Part 2"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-13 09:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "Building on the first part, we dive deeper into MCP's architecture patterns and how they enable scalable, maintainable web applications."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "listingImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "c5efa862-2aa2-49cc-90c5-b46f821beacd",
              "mediaKey": "d5147934-eb93-4115-afd3-350bcfd2e4cd",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "body",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "5835c536-62f6-4b1f-a97a-413ec5805e25",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "eda0d18a-238a-4a87-aa2b-02dc686d1f7b",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "56df2121-8657-40d9-b6ce-00b016148023",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "0ede2580-887a-4ee4-a8a0-9676a0856a92",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "8ece1380-7b5c-4ff8-af21-c0405e60812b",
                "key": "5835c536-62f6-4b1f-a97a-413ec5805e25",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>In Part 1, we introduced the core concepts of MCP. Now let's dive deeper into the practical implementation patterns that make MCP work in real-world Umbraco projects.</p><p>One of the most powerful aspects of MCP is the ability to create reusable <strong>ViewComponents</strong> that encapsulate both the model logic and the presentation layer. These ViewComponents become the building blocks of your entire application.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "7fa92327-7ebc-4c31-91bf-c60dc3fb01fd",
                "key": "eda0d18a-238a-4a87-aa2b-02dc686d1f7b",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "b2c3d4e5-6789-01bc-def0-2345678901bc",
                        "mediaKey": "98bc9fe8-be62-49b4-8b90-60ddbd2010e9",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "caption",
                    "value": "ViewComponent Architecture - showing how models, components, and views connect"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "8ece1380-7b5c-4ff8-af21-c0405e60812b",
                "key": "56df2121-8657-40d9-b6ce-00b016148023",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Key MCP implementation patterns include:</p><ol><li><strong>Block-based ViewComponents</strong>: Each block type has a dedicated ViewComponent that handles both data processing and rendering</li><li><strong>Composition over Inheritance</strong>: Use Umbraco compositions to create reusable content structures without tight coupling</li><li><strong>Service Layer Pattern</strong>: Extract business logic into dedicated services following the *Service naming convention</li><li><strong>Strongly-typed Models</strong>: Generate models from your document types using ModelsBuilder</li><li><strong>ViewModel Mapping</strong>: Create clean ViewModel classes that separate presentation concerns from content models</li></ol>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "ae448a70-e946-44f6-94d0-31218f039712",
                "key": "0ede2580-887a-4ee4-a8a0-9676a0856a92",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "quote",
                    "value": "The real power of MCP comes from its ability to make your codebase more maintainable while simultaneously making it more reusable. It's a win-win for development teams."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "attribution",
                    "value": "Umbraco.Bench Architecture Guide"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "5835c536-62f6-4b1f-a97a-413ec5805e25",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
